WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.670
JIMMY TRAN: Did you
hear Yanny or Laurel?

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.176
I heard Yanny.

00:00:04.176 --> 00:00:05.550
This would be a
great opportunity

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.860
to use the Google
Cloud Speech API to see

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.080
if your suspicions are correct.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.290
The Speech API allows you to
send audio and receive a text

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:17.400
transcription from the
speech-to-text API service.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.500
Learning how to use
this API is easier

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.970
than you might have thought.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.470
In fact, you can
learn how to use it

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.110
in about 15 minutes in Qwiklabs.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.420
And no, you do not need
a Google Cloud Platform

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.060
account or project to
try this for yourself.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.030
An account, project,
and associated resources

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.250
are provided to you as
part of the Qwiklab.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.670
In this lab, you'll create
a file for the request

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.960
to the Speech API.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.460
You'll then see the
transcript value

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.740
return the Speech API's
text transcription

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.210
of your audio file.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.070
The confidence value
indicates how sure

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.480
the API is that it's accurately
transcribed your audio.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.610
And just like that, you've
created a Speech API request.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.060
The Speech API supports both
synchronous and asynchronous

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.310
speech-to-text transcription.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.250
In this example, you sent
it a complete audio file.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.560
But you can also use the
sync recognize method

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.230
to perform streaming
speech-to-text transcription

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.100
while the user is
still speaking.

